3.What does the crossorigin attribute in <img> do?
A)Rotates the image
B)Configures CORS requests for images from other domains
C)Links to a CSS file
D)Sets image contrast

✅ Correct Answer: B. Configures CORS requests for images from other domains

Explanation (Simple)

The crossorigin attribute on an <img> tag controls how the browser makes Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) requests when loading images from another domain.

It is commonly used when:

Loading images from a different website.
Using the image in a <canvas> without "tainting" the canvas.
Example:
<img src="https://example.com/image.png" crossorigin="anonymous" alt="Image">

Common values:

anonymous → Sends the request without user credentials (cookies, HTTP authentication).
use-credentials → Sends the request with credentials.
Why not the others?
❌ A. Rotates the image → Use CSS (transform: rotate()).
✅ B. Configures CORS requests for images from other domains → Correct.
❌ C. Links to a CSS file → Use the <link> tag.
❌ D. Sets image contrast → Use CSS (filter: contrast()).

✅ Correct Answer: B. Configures CORS requests for images from other domains


4.What is the correct way to add a 5px border to an image in HTML (legacy)?
A)border="5"  //answer
B)outline="5"
C)stroke="5"
D)frame="5"


✅ Correct Answer: A. border="5"

Explanation (Simple)

In legacy HTML, the border attribute was used to add a border around an image.

Example (Legacy HTML):

<img src="photo.jpg" border="5" alt="Photo">

This adds a 5-pixel border around the image.

Note

The border attribute is deprecated in HTML5. The modern way is to use CSS:

<img src="photo.jpg" alt="Photo" style="border: 5px solid black;">
Why not the others?
✅ A. border="5" → Correct (legacy HTML).
❌ B. outline="5" → Not a valid HTML attribute.
❌ C. stroke="5" → Used with SVG, not <img>.
❌ D. frame="5" → Not a valid HTML attribute.

✅ Correct Answer: A. border="5"



5.Which format is generally not supported natively by web browsers for display?
A)PNG
B)TIFF  //answer
C)WebP
D)GIF

✅ Correct Answer: B. TIFF

Explanation (Simple)

TIFF (Tagged Image File Format) is generally not supported natively by web browsers for displaying images on web pages.

Common web image formats include:

✅ PNG – Supports transparency.
✅ GIF – Supports simple animations.
✅ WebP – Modern format with good compression.
❌ TIFF – Mainly used for printing, scanning, and professional image editing, not for web display.
Why not the others?
A. PNG → Supported by all modern browsers.
B. TIFF → ✅ Correct. Generally not supported natively for web display.
C. WebP → Supported by modern browsers.
D. GIF → Supported by all major browsers.

✅ Correct Answer: B. TIFF


6.Which of these is a "Lossy" compression format?
A)PNG
B)SVG
C)JPEG  //answer
D)GIF


GIF

✅ Correct Answer: C. JPEG

Explanation (Simple)

JPEG (JPG) uses lossy compression, which means it removes some image data to make the file size much smaller. This may slightly reduce image quality.

Image Formats:
❌ PNG → Lossless compression.
❌ SVG → Vector format (doesn't use lossy compression).
✅ JPEG → Lossy compression.
❌ GIF → Lossless compression (limited to 256 colors).
Best Uses:
JPEG → 📷 Photographs and realistic images.
PNG → Logos and images with transparency.
SVG → Icons, logos, and graphics that need to scale.
GIF → Simple animations.

✅ Correct Answer: C. JPEG


7.Which attribute is used to provide a long description for complex images?
A)alt
B)longdesc //answer
C)desc
D)title

✅ Correct Answer: B. longdesc

Explanation (Simple)

The longdesc attribute was used to provide a long description for complex images such as charts, diagrams, or graphs.

Example (Legacy HTML):

<img src="chart.png" alt="Sales chart" longdesc="chart-description.html">
Why not the others?
❌ A. alt → Provides a short alternative text, not a detailed description.
✅ B. longdesc → Correct. Used for a long description of complex images (legacy HTML).
❌ C. desc → Not a valid HTML attribute.
❌ D. title → Shows a tooltip; it is not a long description.

Note: longdesc is obsolete in HTML5 and is rarely used today. Modern websites typically provide detailed descriptions in nearby text or use ARIA techniques for accessibility.

✅ Correct Answer: B. longdesc


